{"id":"3139477","slug":"patrick-mahomes","name":"Patrick Mahomes","position":"QB","team":"KC","headshot":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/headshots/nfl/players/full/3139477.png","formats":{"ppr-12":{"positionRank":12,"ourRank":90,"vorRound":8,"adp":104.4,"gap":14.4,"projected":248.19,"vor":-3.98,"expectedGames":14.11,"floor":157.01,"median":248.23,"ceiling":339.46,"tier":2,"tierDrop":12.4}},"capsule":{"body":"The dip to 248 projected points is not about the player, it is about the calendar. He has gone 282, 283 and 280 the last three seasons, QB11 both of the last two years, and our 2026 number sits at QB13 mostly because we expect 14 games, not 16. When he plays, he produces: 20.2 a game last year, above the weekly median in 9 of 14 weeks, and the worst week was a 6.3. He cleared 15 points in 9 of his 14 played weeks, and his red-zone share of his own touches led all qualifying quarterbacks at 80%. Andy Reid still calls the plays and Mahomes is still first on the depth chart in Kansas City, so nothing about the situation has turned.\n\nThe worry you can see right in the record is the games. Last season ended after week 15, and the availability range runs from 8 to 17 games. If the bottom lands, your quarterback room takes a hit in the fantasy playoffs, which is exactly when you cannot afford it. Week 16 on is also when a backup stashes costs you a roster spot.\n\nDrafters are taking him around pick 104, and quarterbacks drafted there have scored about 246 with the busts and injuries pooled in. Our number is only 2.3 points above that, so you are not being asked to believe anything aggressive. His ADP actually slid from 104.5 to 108.2 over late September, so the room is drifting away from him, not toward him. We would take him at his ADP and not stress, because if he plays 16 again, 280-plus gets you well clear of replacement.","pulls":[],"writtenAt":"2026-10-13T22:35:19.365Z"},"weekRead":null,"season":{"season":2026,"format":"ppr-12","weeks":18,"through":5,"games":4,"point